  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nAn upper-level low remains centered over SE Colorado per early \nmorning GOES water vapor imagery. Downstream, compact mid-level \nshortwaves continue traversing the Ozarks and Upper/Mid-\nMississippi Valley, providing sufficient synoptic lift for \nshowery precipitation over northern Missouri. Meanwhile, an \nelongated low-level jet (LLJ) axis stretching from the southern \nPlains to the Great Lakes is veering ahead of an approaching, \nslow-moving cold front, driving warm air advection and deep \nisentropic ascent to further bolster rain activity.\n\nBy midday, the southwest U.S. upper-level trough is forecast to\nopen up and eject northeastward into the Mid-Mississippi \nValley, positioning itself parallel to the sluggish surface \nfrontal boundary. Strong upper-level dynamics, combined with a \nplume of rich tropical moisture and focused frontal forcing, \nwill set the stage for multiple rounds of widespread rainfall \nbeginning this afternoon and lasting through early Friday \nmorning.\n\nThe latest 00z REFS LPMM QPF guidance indicates broad totals \nranging from 0.25\" to 0.75\" east of the Illinois River Valley \ninto southeast Illinois. Nevertheless, robust frontogenetical \nforcing and high moisture flux along the slow-moving boundary \nfavor narrow mesoscale rain banding. Underneath these localized \nbands, rainfall accumulations of 2\" to 3\" remain entirely \npossible across the CWA.\n\nDrier conditions will quickly overspread the region from \nnorthwest to southeast Friday morning as the frontal boundary \nexits and an expansive Continental Polar (cP) high pushes east \nacross the Midwest and Great Lakes. Strong post-frontal cold air\nadvection will drive a pronounced airmass change, replacing \nearly-week warmth with seasonal fall conditions. Highs on Friday\nand Saturday will drop into the upper 60s, while overnight lows\nfall into the 40s. Ensemble models show high confidence that \nthis expansive Canadian surface high will remain entrenched \nthrough early next week, prolonging a period of tranquil, crisp \nautumn weather.\n\nA warming trend takes hold by mid-week as a potent mid-level \ntrough digs from the Canadian Prairies toward the Great Lakes, \nshifting the surface high eastward. Southward veering surface \nwinds ahead of an advancing cold front will subsequently boost \ntemperatures back into the mid- to upper-70s.",
